[FINAL-TEXT] The evidence is now definitive. Between my two polls the adjacency up-times advanced by exactly ~3 minutes and 22 seconds — consistent with uninterrupted sessions. The entire OSPF database sequence numbers are unchanged (no new LSA updates on any router), and all Dead timers remain healthy at ~35–36 seconds. The network is stable.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
A full inspection of the OSPF control plane across every core and distribution device in the topology — r1, csw1, csw2, dswa1, dswa2, dswb1, dswb2 — shows no fault. Every adjacency is in the Full state with healthy Dead timers (35–38 s against a 40 s deadline, normal for a 10 s Hello interval). Per-neighbor state-change counters read 5–6 on every pair, which is the exact number expected from a single successful adjacency formation (Down→Init→2-Way→ExStart→Exchange→Loading→Full). The "most recent state change" timestamps on all neighbors were 1.5–2.5 minutes ago, coinciding with the lab's OSPF startup/convergence window; no state change has occurred since then.

A second snapshot taken ~3.5 minutes later confirms stability: the adjacency up-timers on r1 (to csw1 and csw2) advanced continuously from ~51 s to ~4 m 13 s with no reset, and every Router-LSA and Network-LSA sequence number in the OSPF database was identical between the two polls, meaning no topology event occurred. Interface error counters on r1, csw1, and csw2 are all zero. Running configurations show standard timers (Hello 10 s, Dead 40 s) with no BFD, no authentication, and no cost mismatches. The telemetry collector is not running and could not provide historical data, but the live state is unambiguous.

The root cause is that no fault exists: the vague "flapping" alert was almost certainly triggered by the transient OSPF convergence chatter at lab startup, not by a real fault. No device or link needs to be restarted.
=== END ===